  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over Wisconsin into Lake\nSuperior, while high over Saskatchewan stretches into eastern\nMontana. Upper level analysis places compact low along the\nManitoba/North Dakota border with trough dipping to the south. A\nvariety of weak waves wrapping around the low continue to\nproduce some light snow over parts of north central North\nDakota, with a few instances of a little snow working its way a\nbit further south towards the northern James River Valley.\n\nFor today, some light snow may linger over north central North\nDakota into the upper James River Valley through early this\nmorning, but should then quickly taper off later morning as the\nlow works its way into northern Minnesota. Thereafter, a rather\nquiet day is on tap. Cooler air wrapping into the area will keep\ntemperatures around 5 to 10 degrees below average. \n\nFor the upcoming week, ensembles continue to indicate mainly dry\nweather is on tap, though some individual deterministic models \ndo indicate some of the random disturbances moving through the \narea could bring a little light precipitation in the \nTuesday/Wednesday time frame. Will have to keep an eye on this \nto see if some precipitation chances will need to eventually be \nadded. Cooler temperatures remain into Monday, then will be on a\ngradual rebound, with highs in the 30s and 40s for Thanksgiving\nDay.",
